    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i checked the drainage and it seems fine. i'm a bit worried though, like it feels like something is actively stripping the nutrients out of the soil faster than i can add them. is there anything else that could be causing that kind of rapid depletion?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A few things could cause rapid depletion beyond just plant uptake. Heavy feeding tomatoes in hot weather can slurp nitrogen fast, but if it feels unusually quick, check for these culprits: excessive rainfall or irrigation leaching nutrients below the root zone, soil pH that's off (below 6.0 or above 7.0 locks up nutrients even if they're present), or bertender competition from weeds or nearby trees stealing nutrients.\n\nIf depletion keeps outpacing your additions, get a soil test—many extension offices do them cheaply. It'll tell you pH, nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium levels so you're treating the actual problem instead of guessing.",
